# Mathematics is the primary specification

The R API falls out of the mathematics. Do not invent a new objective, gauge, transformation group, missingness model, or optimality claim from convenience.

## Canonical sources (read before editing solvers or fits)

1. [docs/spec/00-mathematics.md](docs/spec/00-mathematics.md) — primary specification (42 sections).
2. [docs/spec/00-conventions.md](docs/spec/00-conventions.md) — implementer-facing names and correspondence rules.
3. [docs/spec/01-solver-guarantee-matrix.md](docs/spec/01-solver-guarantee-matrix.md) — the only allowed optimality claims.
4. [inst/CHARTER.md](inst/CHARTER.md) — product charter and non-goals.

## Non-negotiable laws

- Rows are entities, columns are dimensions; right action \(T(X)=sXR+\mathbf{1}t\).
- \(O(d)\) and \(SO(d)\) are different groups, not a Boolean afterthought.
- Estimation returns an equivalence class; canonicalization is a later presentation.
- “Certificate unavailable” never becomes “probably global.”
- Missing rows are not missing cells. Implicit sparse zeros are never missing.
- Superimposition metric \(\Sigma_S\) and model covariance \(\Sigma_M\) are different objects.
- Implementation uses solves and moments, never explicit inverses or `scale(X)` densification.

If an implementation disagrees with the mathematics document, the mathematics document wins.
